    "passage": "Have you ever been bombarded with various criticisms from different people but chosen to ignore them and insist that you are right? In this case, you may have experienced the Dunning-Kruger effect. The concept of the Dunning-Kruger effect [[1]] in a 1999 paper by Cornell University psychologists David Dunning and Justin Kruger. The participants took a test assessing their logic, grammar, and sense of humor, and the results revealed that those who performed poorly [[2]] their skills as far above average.\nThe Dunning-Kruger effect is a(n) [[3]] bias in which individuals tend to overestimate their knowledge or ability in a specific area. Two main [[4]] can explain this. Firstly, lacking metacognition, people describe themselves as above average in intelligence, humor, and other skills, which restrains them from [[5]] their real capabilities and learning progress. Thus, they can’t acknowledge their incompetence. Secondly, this [[6]] shows that some people lack the self-awareness to evaluate their skills. As a(n) [[7]], after they acquire a small amount of knowledge in a particular field, the phenomenon occurs. They are satisfied with the content they’ve obtained, which [[8]] inappropriate confidence. This inflated confidence makes them feel like experts suddenly.\nTo avoid this effect, people should make a constant effort to learn about a new topic and challenge themselves to consider that their [[9]] might be wrong. Only after continuing to explore a new subject do they realize how extensive it is and how far they should go before mastering it. In addition, people ought to seek [[10]] from peers while learning so as not to become overconfident. Following these tips can help people avoid falling into the trap of the Dunning-Kruger effect.",
